How to read it. Each bar is a stock’s fitted mean, split into the factor part (blue) and the characteristic-intercept part (gold); dots mark the total. The table gives the root-mean-square intercept part, in basis points a month, for each mean against each space; the diagonal cells are each model’s own alpha. Source: illustrative numbers chosen for this page, not estimates from the paper; the layout follows the paper’s Table 1.


The Mean slider sets how much of Model A’s extra, non-factor signal Model B’s fitted mean keeps. The Space slider lets Model B’s factor absorb that extra signal. At the defaults, B’s alpha is half of A’s (13.4 against 26.7 bp). Along the first row, B’s mean in A’s space gives the same drop; down the first column, B’s space leaves A’s residual unchanged. So the mean changed. Now set both sliders to 1. B’s alpha falls to zero, but now the first column drops and the first row does not: B’s factor explains more. The diagonal looks alike in both cases; only the crossing tells them apart.

5 A simple example
Take two characteristics, A and B, say two value measures, with cross-sectional correlation ρ. Under the plain ruler their coefficient vectors are perpendicular, so a Euclidean model can put A in the latent block and B in the benchmark block; under the data’s ruler they overlap by ρ. Let the priced direction load equally on A and B. Drag the slider towards 0 and the two rulers agree; drag it up and the two “separate” blocks together claim more than 100 percent of the priced variance, because the overlap is counted twice. Separating B from A in the data’s ruler gives B only what A leaves unexplained, and the total returns to 100 percent. In U.S. data some pairs of characteristics correlate at 0.94 (paper, Table 5).


