How to read it. Illustrative example, not the paper’s data. Characteristic X (theme X) and characteristic Z (theme Z) are standardized, have correlation ρ, and each has a true coefficient of 0.5. A ridge rule with penalty λ is fitted on population moments. Copying X k times with an ordinary penalty on each copy is the same as cutting X’s penalty to λ/k (Section I.B, equation 2). The solid line shows theme X’s share of fitted payoff variance, as in equation 4; the dashed line applies the transported penalty. “Error above best fit” is the population prediction error in excess of the unpenalized fit. Source: computed in the browser from the formulas in Sections I.B and I.D of the paper; no data file.


Start at k = 1: the two themes get equal weight and each explains half the fitted variance. Now raise the copies slider: the data do not change, yet weight moves from Z to X and X’s share reaches 73% at the default settings, while the dashed transported control stays at 50%. The prediction error in the last column falls as copies are added, because in this population toy any positive penalty is too strong and the copies weaken it.
In finite samples with retuning, copies can help or hurt (Section V.A), so a better test score is not proof of new information (paper, Section V.C; Table VII). Raise λ and the shift grows; with a tiny λ the copies hardly matter. Here λ is held fixed; the paper chooses it again, and the shift survives because one penalty number cannot undo copies of a single theme (Section I.B).

