How to read it. Illustrative example with made-up numbers, not estimates from the paper. Stock 1 loads on factor 1 (mean 0.01, variance 0.02, loading 1); stock 2 loads on an independent factor whose units never change. Each stock has residual variance 0.01 and a stock-level ridge of 0.01. Rescaling factor 1 by k multiplies its mean by k and divides the loading by k. This is the simplest kind of change a ruler can make; a real change of ruler rescales and mixes many exposures at once (Slides 10, A13). Source: illustrative arithmetic on the portfolio rule in the paper (slides 8 and 10); no data files.


In this made-up example, at k = 2 the forecast (0.01) and the factor risk (0.02) do not move, but the ridge charge falls from 0.01 to 0.0025, so stock 1’s share rises from 50 to about 54 percent. Drag the slider: larger units shrink the penalty and tilt the book toward stock 1. Then switch on “Transport the ridge with the units”: the split stays at 50/50 for every k. That is the paper’s remedy (Slides 16, A13).

